engagement and enjoyment.'], [[523, 85, 540, 173], 'sec_1', 'ABSTRACT'], [[543, 85, 723, 484], 'para', 'This paper compares the effectiveness of data comics and
infographics for data-driven storytelling. While infograph-
ics are widely used, comics are increasingly popular for ex-
plaining complex and scientific concepts. However, empirical
evidence comparing the effectiveness and engagement of in-
fographics, comics and illustrated texts is still lacking. We
report on the results of two complementary studies, one in a
controlled setting and one in the wild. Our results suggest
participants largely prefer data comics in terms of enjoyment,
focus, and overall engagement and that comics improve un-
derstanding and recall of information in the stories. Our'], [[525, 516, 572, 914], 'para', 'findings help to understand the respective roles of the inves-
tigated formats as well as inform the design of more effective
data comics and infographics.'], [[582, 516, 599, 615], 'sec_1', 'KEYWORDS'], [[602, 516, 618, 865], 'para', 'Visualization, Comics, Effectiveness, Engagement'], [[626, 516, 640, 679], 'sec_1', 'ACM Reference Format:'], [[640, 516, 723, 915], 'para', 'Zezhong Wang, Shunning Wang, Matteo Farinella, Dave Murray-Rust,
